        ":question/worked-solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=7$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x+2\\cdot 7\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x+14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=10,$ then $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x+14\\pi )=10.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x+14\\pi )+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}(10)+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2}+6 \\\\  & =5+6 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
